Effect of luminance of head-up displays on recognition of visual objects on roads
Takashi Hosokawa a,*, Hiroshi Hashimoto a, Takayuki Tsui b, Keisuke Saito b, Tomotaka Igarashi b, Akinari Hirao c
a Japan Automobile Research Institute, Karima 2530, Tsukuba, Ibaraki 305-0822 Japan b Japan Automobile Manufacturers Association, Inc, Jidosha Kaikan (NBF Tower) 16th Floor1-30, Shiba Daimon 1-chome Minato-ku, Tokyo 105-0012 Japan c Shibaura Institute of Technology, Toyosu 3-7-5, Koto, Tokyo 135-8548 Japan

ARTICLE INFO
Keywords: Head-up display Luminance Distraction Driver recognition Augmented reality Field of view

ABSTRACT
Objective: The purpose of this study is to investigate the effect of visual obstruction when a head-up display (HUD) is presented in front of a driver's field of view. Background: The use of HUDs is expected to increase due to advances in augmented reality (AR) technology. A conventional HUD is displayed at a relatively low position in relation to the driver's viewpoint and has little effect on their front view. However, while an HUD, such as the AR-HUD, is presented in front of the driver's field of vision and helps them in presenting ADAS information, there is a concern that this display may obscure visual objects on the road from the driver's field of vision. Method: An experiment using a mockup consisting of a HUD and a driver's seat was conducted with 28 partic­ ipants. A passenger car, motorcycle, and crossing pedestrian were presented as visual objects on a 65-in monitor behind the image of the HUD. Participants were asked questions under different conditions with changes in the HUD luminance and asked to correctly answer questions regarding changes in the inter-vehicle distance and the existence of visual objects on the road. The number of correct answers provided by the participants was recorded. Results: The experimental results revealed that the HUD had an effect under the twilight condition wherein the background luminance was low. Furthermore, analyzing the number of correct answers for each visual target revealed that the HUD had an effect under conditions wherein the size of the visual object was small. Conclusion: It was experimentally derived that the luminance ratio Rluminance = 1.1 was the threshold value that affected the recognition of the visual object in the background when the HUD was presented in front of the driver's field of view. Implication: The threshold value of the luminance ratio Rluminance obtained in this study can be considered when deciding how to properly display the HUD in front of the driver's field of view.

1. Introduction
Head-up displays (HUDs) are increasingly being installed in auto­ mobiles. The idea of a HUD is that superimposes an imaginary image of an instrument on an image of the outside world was first proposed in the 1940s. It was not until 1960 that HUDs were produced for use in military aircrafts, while now are widely used in commercial aircrafts as well. In automobiles, they began to be used in the mid-1980s, and are now used in a wide range of vehicles [1]. Since around 1990, the effects of HUDs have been compared with those of head-down displays [2,3]. Compared to conventional instrument panels, HUDs have the advantage of making

it easier for drivers to check road conditions and maintain lane control [4].
Current HUDs are placed below the driver's field of view and present information on navigation routes, vehicle speeds, and road signs. The evolution of augmented reality (AR) technology is expected to further popularize them. By applying AR, information can be obtained by overlaying the HUD on the vehicle in front, pedestrian crossing, or display along the curbline [5,6]. Furthermore, the entire windshield can be used as a display area for HUD [7]. A study comparing the effec­ tiveness of AR in visual and auditory modalities, not only for providing information, but also for presenting warnings, has shown the

