effectiveness and acceptability of visual AR displays [8]. Studies inves­ tigating pedestrian crossings and road signs have also shown that many drivers do not recognize pedestrian crossings and drive above the maximum speed limit of the signs, and that it is possible to intervene from the infrastructure side by making pedestrian crossings and signs more visually prominent [9]. Such visual enhancement can be achieved not only from the infrastructure side, such as painting the road, but also by AR-HUD, and enhancement by AR-HUD is expected to be effective. In addition, AR-HUD with a sense of depth can increase the driver's attention span and thus reduce the workload compared with normal HUD displays [10]. Therefore, AR-HUDs offer several advantages. Because it is presented in front of the driver's field of view more than a conventional HUD, it is expected to be more effective in presenting information.
However, there are concerns that this may cause visual obstruction. For example, when a HUD display is filled with navigational informa­ tion, the background of the display may not be sufficiently transparent to allow another car on the road to be clearly seen [11]. If a motorcycle is behind a filled HUD image, the driver may not see it until it is too late. One study investigated the preferred position of HUD in an actual driving environment; many experimental participants preferred the HUD in the visual field below the line of sight and outside the traffic scene [12]. For these reasons, an AR-HUD must be optimized such that it is both effective as a display and safe to use without creating any visual obstructions to the driver's field of view [13].
Currently, UN Regulation No. 125, the international regulation for the forward field of vision of automobile drivers, defines a HUD that is presented in front of the driver as a field of view assistant (FVA), rather than a conventional HUD, and discusses its requirements. There are situations in which quantitative indicators are required for FVA.
The purpose of this study is to investigate the effect of the HUD on the forward field of view of the driver. To this end, we focused on HUD luminance and conducted an experimental study on a display luminance that could achieve both proper visibility and safety for drivers. A bench test using a HUD was conducted in the darkroom of the Japan Auto­ mobile Research Institute (JARI). A mockup consisting of the HUD and driver's seat in Fig. 1 was constructed and set up to observe a 65-in monitor located 5.0 m from the forward field of view of the driver. An actual traffic scene in the background was projected on this monitor and observed through a HUD image adjusted to constant luminance; the effect of HUD luminance on the ability of the driver to recognize the traffic scene in the background was then investigated. Fig. 2 shows the flowchart of the analysis in this study. First, we compared daytime and twilight conditions. Next, by analyzing the number of correct answers for each visual object, we derived the upper limit threshold for HUD luminance.

