2. Experiment participants and apparatus used 2.1. Participants
There were 28 participants, 14 men and 14 women, in the experi­ ment. The men were aged 21–66 (mean 42.1 years, SD 14.5 years) and the women were aged 21–66 (mean 42.9 years, SD 14.3). To ensure uniformity in the age distribution of the participants, we selected three people in their 20s to 50s and two people in their 60s belonging to both genders. All participants had a driver's license and drove daily. Further, they were recruited from the general public, were not engaged in the research and development of automobiles or HUDs, and had no expe­ rience using HUDs on a daily basis. This study was approved by the Experimental Ethics Committee of the Japan Automobile Research Institute (JARI) and informed consent was obtained from all participants.
The purpose of the experiment was explained to the participants using an instruction sheet, and they were asked to confirm their char­ acteristics, such as age and gender, their driving experience, and whether they had previously used an HUD. Before starting the experi­ mental task, a practice task was conducted using scenes not used in the experiment. This endured that the participants understood the task before performing it.
2.2. Apparatus The traffic scene in the background was displayed on a 65-in 4 K
resolution liquid crystal display (TCL 65C601U), as shown in Fig. 1. The distance between the center of the display and the ocular point was set to 5.0 m. For the HUD, a projector unit with a digital micromirror device

