- After counting from three to one, two still images were displayed in succession with a blackout interval, accompanied by a beep signal.
- There were two possible changes in the inter-vehicle distance: it was either increased or decreased by 10 m.
- The presentation times of the first and second images were set to 0.7 and 0.3 s, respectively.
- The blackout interval was set because the time when the intervehicle distance changed due to a deceleration of 0.4 g (3.92 m/s2) in 10 m was 2.27 s. The deceleration of 0.4 g was set because it is often used as a threshold for strong braking [17–19].

To evaluate the effect of HUDs on the forward visibility of a person, we evaluated the extent to which participants could comprehend the traffic situation in front of a vehicle when given a very short time to observe it. For this purpose, traffic scene images were presented for a short period of time and the ability of the participants to discriminate between them was examined. We included three types of visual objects in a traffic scene: passenger cars, motorcycles, and crossing pedestrians. When the passenger car and motorcycle were the visual targets, we asked the participants to answer questions regarding changes in the distance between the two still images before and after HUD luminance decreased. When a pedestrian was the visual target, we asked for an­ swers regarding the presence or absence of crossing pedestrians and the crossing direction in one still image. Fig. 3 shows the time and flow for presenting still images of passenger cars, motorcycles, and crossing pedestrians.
Regarding still images presented for a short period, if they were presented for too long, the difference in driving decisions may have been too large, and it would have been easier to identify the differences from reality. However, if the presentation time was too short, it would have been difficult to fully comprehend what was presented. In a study that analyzed the gaze behavior of drivers under three types of traffic situ­ ations, the average gazing time of drivers on the road ahead ranged from 0.7 to 1.5 s [14]. In another study, the average gazing times of novice and expert drivers involved in visual searches were approximately 0.3 and 0.5 s, respectively [15]. Another study that analyzed the gazing behavior of drivers showed that the average gazing time of drivers for visual information in front of them on the road varied, ranging from 0.24 to 1.03 s [16].
From these studies, we inferred that the minimum time for a driver to recognize visual information on the road ahead would be approximately 0.3 s. Below, we discuss the evaluation items and presentation methods, followed by different visual targets.
