Evaluation items: Change in inter-vehicle distance. Presentation method:

(2) Visual target: Pedestrian
Evaluation items: Existence of pedestrian and crossing direction. Presentation method:
- After counting from three to one, one still image was presented along with a beep signal.
- There were three possible conditions for the pedestrians: they either crossed in a direction from the right or left hand of the vehicle or did not cross at all.
- The image presentation time was set to 0.3 s.
For passenger cars and motorcycles, the initial inter-vehicle initial distances were set at 30 and 50 m, respectively, with or without a change in distance (10 m shortened (deceleration), unchanged, or 10 m increased (acceleration)). Three types of pedestrians were selected: those who crossed from the left, those who crossed from the right, or those who did not cross at all. In addition to daylight conditions, wherein the visual targets were easier to distinguish, we also set a twi­ light condition, wherein the brightness of the images was reduced. We focused on whether the experimental participants noticed the sudden deceleration of the passenger car or motorcycle in front of the vehicle. Accordingly, the change in inter-vehicle distance was based on two patterns: no change or short change. However, if there were only two patterns, it would be easy for participants to predict them; therefore, we added some conditions to increase the inter-vehicle distance and make the prediction more challenging.
Visibility through HUD may also depend on the content displayed. Currently, HUDs commonly display content such as speed and naviga­ tion arrows, but in the future, they may be used to highlight objects on the road. All these can distract the driver's attention but are unlikely to significantly obstruct their field of view. However, if the entire display area of the HUD were to light up brightly owing to a malfunction or for some other reason, it could obstruct an extremely wide field of view compared to speed and navigation displays. Assuming this worst-case scenario, this study examined the brightness requirements that would allow background objects to be recognized when the HUD is displayed over such a wide field of view. If the requirements for visibility in this worst-case scenario are clarified, we believe that visibility will be ensured, even if a large area is rendered by AR-HUDs in the future. Therefore, we filled the entire HUD with a single white color and set the display luminance from 0 to 500 cd/m2 in increments of 100 cd/m2. The virtual image distance of the HUD was set to 5 m, which was equidistant from the LCD, and the HUD was presented to overlap the visual object.
We evaluated the number of correct answers that participants pro­ vided under 156 conditions. The combinations of these conditions are shown in Table 1 and examples of the visual targets are shown in Figs. 4–6.

