4.2. Analysis of the number of correct answers for each visual target
A two-way ANOVA confirmed the effects of HUD luminance under twilight conditions. Therefore, we analyzed the differences in the number of correct answers for each visual target (passenger car, motorcycle, and pedestrian) under twilight conditions. Chi-square analysis was used to analyze the relationship between the HUD lumi­ nance and the number of correct/false answers for each of the 13 twi­ light conditions. The following three conditions showed significant relationships in the chi-square analysis (p < 0.001): twilight/motor­ cycle/50 m/increased, twilight/pedestrian/50 m/crossing from the right, and twilight/pedestrian/50 m/crossing from the left. Figs. 9–11 show the number of correct answers under these three conditions; the HUD luminance condition for which the p-value of the adjusted stan­ dardized residuals was significant as a result of the residual analysis and the subject's visual perception when the HUD luminance was 500 cd/m2, the most difficult viewing condition.
Under low-light conditions, the effect of HUD was not observed when a passenger car was the visual target or when the distance to the motorcycle remained the same or decreased. On the other hand, the three conditions under which there were significant differences due to the HUD were those in which the distance to the motorcycle increased and those in which the pedestrians crossed the street. This indicates that the HUD had an effect when the size of the visual object was small and luminance was low.
