the number of participants and the male-to-female ratio were almost the same. In addition, the number of participants in the studies that con­ ducted evaluation experiments using HUDs with experimental partici­ pants was 30 for [25], 24 for [26], 30 for [27], 10 for [28], and 30 for [29]. The number of participants in this study (28) is similar in similar experiments. The small number of participants in the experiment is a limitation of this study and we believe that the statistical treatment ensures the validity of the results to some extent; however, it is possible that unobserved heterogeneity remains: all experimental participants were Japanese, that is, all had brown irises. It is known that differences in iris color have a significant effect on intraocular stray light, especially in low-light conditions, which affects glare sensitivity [30]. As such ef­ fects were not examined in this study, future validation experiments with a larger number of participants are required.
6. Conclusions
This study examined the luminance effects of a HUD on the presence, relative change in distance, and crossing direction of visual objects, including a passenger car, motorcycle, and pedestrian, in front of the driver in a traffic environment presented on a 65-in. LCD monitor for a short time. The HUD image, which was white on all sides, was largely presented in the field of view in front of the driver to avoid the driver being affected by the presented content. The luminance varied from 0 cd/m2 (“no HUD” condition) to 500 cd/m2 in increments of 100 cd/m2.
A comparison between daylight and twilight conditions showed that the rate of correct answers provided by the participants was significantly lower under twilight conditions. Under daylight conditions, even with the highest HUD luminance of 500 cd/m2, there was no significant difference in the movement of the visual target or in the rate of correct answers for detection. However, under twilight conditions, significant

