differences in the aforementioned factors were observed when HUD luminance was 200 cd/m2 or higher.
Under twilight conditions, we compared the number of correct an­ swers for each visual target, finding significant differences in the con­ ditions of motorcycles and pedestrians 50 m away. We derived the luminance ratios for the three conditions in which there was a signifi­ cant difference and examined the threshold value.
By using Eq. (2), we determined the threshold of HUD luminance to avoid affecting the recognition of visual targets when the HUD was presented in front of the driver's field of view. The threshold value of LHUD was calculated by substituting the luminance of the real environ­ ment into Eq. (2). This threshold value was sufficiently high under daylight conditions, indicating that the effect of HUD was limited. However, when the luminance of the visual target was low, such as after sunset, the effect of HUD luminance has to be considered.
This study led to the development of a HUD luminance threshold that affects the discrimination of visual objects seen in front of the driver when the HUD is presented in front of the driver's field of vision. When applying this threshold to a real environment, the problem of defining the luminance values of these objects and that of the background surface (the road) must be considered. Currently, there is no definition of standard luminance for visible objects such as passenger cars, motor­ cycles, and pedestrians on roads or a standard luminance for road sur­ faces. In the future, these luminances should be defined and the influence of luminance under low-light conditions, such as twilight, should be investigated.
